# Operator secrets for the scripts in scripts/ and the Claude skills in .claude/.
#
#   cp .env.ops.example .env.ops      # .env.ops is git-ignored; never commit it
#   edit .env.ops
#
# This file holds SECRETS AND TOKENS ONLY. Nothing else belongs here:
#   - Cloudflare resource names and IDs (Worker, D1, R2, account ID) live in src/server/wrangler.toml.
#   - Event name, sender, GitHub repo, site/CDN URLs, report bucket, attendance sheet ID live in site.config.json.
# Scripts read this file themselves (no `source` needed). A variable exported in your shell
# with the same name wins over the line here. Run `make doctor` to check the whole setup.
#
# The Worker's own secrets (JWT_SECRET, MAILGUN_*, GOOGLE_SA_KEY) live in
# src/server/.dev.vars for local dev (see src/server/.dev.vars.example) and are set with
# `wrangler secret put` in Cloudflare. The scripts that need GOOGLE_* or MAILGUN_* look here
# first and then in src/server/.dev.vars, so you do not have to copy them.

# ── Cloudflare AGENT token (required for any remote read) ────────────────────
# A token scoped as narrowly as Cloudflare allows, for day-to-day diagnostics by you or an AI agent:
#   D1: read, R2: read, Workers: Tail read. Nothing that edits or deploys.
# Cloudflare may not let a D1 read-only token run queries (not verified).
# If you had to grant D1 Edit to the agent token, that token can write to production: the read guard covers only d1-query, not direct wrangler commands, so rely on the agent rules in ADMIN.md section 8. See .claude/skills/devops/SKILL.md, "Tokens".
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=

# The DEPLOY token (Workers, Pages, D1 edit) does NOT go in this file. In CI it is the GitHub
# Actions secret CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN. To deploy from your machine, read it without echo for one command:
#   read -rs CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN && export CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN && scripts/deploy.sh stg server; unset CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN
# A person runs this, not an AI agent.
# scripts/deploy.sh reads it from the process environment only.

# ── Google (optional features) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# To get these: in the Google Cloud console create a project, enable the Google Sheets API,
# create a service account and download its JSON key. Then share each sheet with the service
# account's email address (client_email in the JSON; Editor for the committee todo sheet).
# Service-account key, base64 of the JSON (base64 < service-account.json). Used by sync_attendance.py,
# compute-awards-v2.py, committee-todo-cli.py. Unsetting it turns off both the prediction
# contest data and the committee todo page, because both read their sheets through this key.
GOOGLE_SA_KEY=
# Prediction-contest sheet ID (compute-awards-v2.py): the ID in the sheet URL between /d/ and /edit.
GOOGLE_SPREADSHEET_ID=
# Committee to-do sheet ID (committee-todo-cli.py).
GOOGLE_COMMITTEE_SHEET_ID=
# The attendance sheet ID (sync_attendance.py) is not a secret: set it in site.config.json
# (sheets.attendance.spreadsheetId).

# ── Email (optional) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Mailgun credentials for the test-email script
# (npm -w @smriti/server run spike:mailgun:confirmation). Needs a Mailgun account and a sending
# domain whose DNS records (SPF, DKIM) Mailgun shows as verified. The deployed Worker gets its own
# copy of these with `wrangler secret put` (docs/DEPLOYMENT.md, step 4); a deployed site
# without Mailgun has no working login.
MAILGUN_API_KEY=
MAILGUN_DOMAIN=

# ── Image generation (optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Gemini API key for scripts/imagegen (paid API; `imagegen run` is capped by --max-spend).
GEMINI_API_KEY=
